const scriptCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Four ways to write', note: 'Alphabets (Latin: 1 letter ≈ 1 sound), abjads (Arabic: consonants written, vowels optional), syllabaries (Japanese kana: 1 symbol = 1 syllable), logographies (Chinese: 1 character = 1 word/morpheme). Each is a different theory of what language is made of.' },
    { math: '2 · Chinese characters', note: '~50,000 characters exist; ~3,000 cover reading (educated readers know 4,000–8,000). Characters encode meaning + sound partially (radicals + phonetic components). Learning curve: years — and it shapes reading cognition differently (more right-hemisphere involvement).' },
    { math: '3 · Devanagari and abugidas', note: 'Consonants carry the base symbol; vowels attach as marks (क + ि = कि). Used across South Asia for hundreds of languages — a middle system: sound-based but syllable-shaped.' },
    { math: '4 · Hangul: the designed script', note: 'Korea\'s Hangul (1443) was ENGINEERED: letter shapes mimic mouth positions, and letters combine into syllable blocks. Created by King Sejong to raise literacy — and it worked (Korean literacy ~99%).' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/languages/advanced/writing-systems-of-the-world">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Writing systems are <strong>humanity's encoding standards for speech</strong> — four designs
    (alphabets, abjads, syllabaries, logographies), each with trade-offs in learning time,
    fidelity and beauty. Learning a new script is learning a new visual system, and it is one of
    language learning's most satisfying milestones.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four system types and their flagship scripts" widget="equationStep" config={scriptCfg} />

  <h2>Practical script-learning notes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Kana in a weekend:</strong> hiragana + katakana (92 symbols) are learnable in 1–2 weeks with mnemonics — the gateway that unlocks Japanese reading structure early.</li>
    <li><strong>Stroke order matters in CJK scripts:</strong> it trains muscle memory, legibility and dictionary lookup — the traditional order exists for motor efficiency.</li>
    <li><strong>Arabic script reads right to left</strong> with letters that change shape by position (initial/medial/final/isolated) — the shape-shifting is systematic, learnable in days, and cursive by nature.</li>
    <li><strong>Scripts affect cognition subtly:</strong> Chinese readers process characters holistically; alphabetic readers assemble phonologically — reading science's cross-script findings (the reading brain differs by script, without one being better).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Coulmas, The Writing Systems of the World; Sampson, Writing Systems; King Sejong's Hunminjeongeum (1446).</p>
</TopicPage>
